{
  "dom_id": "step-q124095",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "${\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)}=$",
  "worked_solution": "First, note that the series can be written as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{n(n+1)}.\n$$\n\nUsing partial fractions we can write the series as\n\n$$\n\\frac{4}{3}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{n}-\\frac{1}{n+1}).\n$$\n\nComputing the first few partial sums, starting with $n=5,$ gives\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{6})] \\\\ {s}_{6} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\frac{1}{7})] \\\\ {s}_{7} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\frac{1}{8})] \\\\ {s}_{8} & =\\frac{4}{3}[(\\frac{1}{5}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{6}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{7}}-\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}})+(\\cancel{\\frac{1}{8}}-\\frac{1}{9})].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that for each partial sum, we always end up canceling all terms with the exception of the first and last terms. Therefore, the $N$th partial sum is\n\n$$\n{s}_{N}=\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}].\n$$\n\nFinally, we can determine the sum of the series by taking the limit of ${s}_{N}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=5}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{3n(n+1)} & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }{s}_{N} \\\\  & ={lim}_{N\\to \\infty }\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-\\frac{1}{N+1}] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}[\\frac{1}{5}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
